Z||000000||Voyd||12-22-1999||10:32 PM||||does the Fiero have a block heater plug? mine sometimes spends a few days outside in the freezing weather and id like to plug it in. i really tried finding that plug but i just couldnt see it.<P>thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-23-1999||12:00 AM||||Even living where you do, I wouldn't worry too much about it.  If you feel you need one, try JC Whitney.  They have just about everything automotive.  (As a matter of fact, I believe that's their motto.)||||reg||1
Z||000002||dogtired||12-23-1999||07:35 AM||||Voyd, I don't believe they do. But I would just like to jump in here on your thread and see what other heater havers have?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>On older cars there was one type you could add on into your heater hose and it would circulate water through the block and heater core. Now newer cars like ours, I understand that since the heater and A/C are on the same system you have to put a Y fitting in that hose???? Then there is always the stick it down the dip stick hole? Not sure these do much?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Surley someone on this great forum uses accessory heat from time to time?? and could shead a little light on the subject???   THANKS<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Merry Christmas to all, and to all, a good night!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000003||deceler8||12-23-1999||11:05 AM||||Probably the best heater is the magnetic one that sticks to the oil pan or block. Doesn't require any installation, is easily removed, and keeps the oil warm, which does more for starting than keeping the coolant warm.<P>Or switch to Mobil 1 0W-30 in the coldest months. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||William Federle||12-23-1999||11:12 AM||||My 1984 SE has a block heater that is mounted<BR>in a freeze plug but it trips the circuit<BR>breaker in my house when I plug it in. I've<BR>been thinking of getting the magnetic mount<BR>heater but I thought I might have a clearance<BR>problem if I left it attached - the picture of it shows something like a handle protruding down a couple of inches when it's<BR>attached to the bottom of the oil pan - has anyone used it and is this just how it looks<BR>in the picture or is it really like that?<BR>(The high today in Milwaukee is 15 deg so<BR>I'd like to find out soon.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||12-23-1999||03:24 PM||||I have to use 10W40 in my engine.  On start up the pressure goes to nearly 80psi, so the stuff is pretty thick.  I would like to know more about the magnetic mount heater.  It sounds like that's the way to go.||||reg||1
Z||000006||olympic||12-23-1999||03:40 PM||||  Here in Saskatchewan, a block heater is not  an option. At -40(its the same in farenheit and celcius) absolutely nothing sitting outside will start. The inline circulating type that dogtired is talking about is used on large engines like diesels. I wouldn't use one on a small V6 of I4 because they put out so much heat that they could actually boil the coolant. The most common one is the type that goes in one of the freeze plugs in the block. They work fine and my car will start at any temp when its plugged in. Many people install 2 block heaters on a V8 engine, one on each bank of cylinders. <BR>    As for the magnetic oil heaters, your wasting your money. They may heat the oil but you would be way further ahead to just use a 100% synthetic oil in the winter. Around here you can buy 0w30 grade oil. It's great and stays pourable to -50. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||12-26-1999||08:34 PM||||olympic--All of the heaters I've ever used on a big truck (and I've driven many of them) have been block heaters.  I've never had a circulating type on a truck.  <P>If anyone uses a heater, make sure you plug it in while the car is still warm.  It takes less energy that way.  Your electric bill will thank you.  (Once again, I know this from experience. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000008||olympic||12-27-1999||01:55 AM||||  I don't know much about semi-tractors but I've seen lots of circulating heaters on older farm tractors. Come to think of it I havn't seen a circulating heater in a long time. The new tractors I've seen all come with block heaters. I had the circulating type in my first car(a 65 Pontiac with a straight six). I could plug it in for 2 hours at -30C and the coolant temp would be +60C(140F). The heater would actually blow hot air as soon as you started it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||2FAST4U||12-27-1999||02:38 AM||||Block heaters are the way to go, but are a real pain in the but tu install in a fiero with the engine in the car because of clearance problems getting the old freeze plug out and the new heater in without damaging it. Oil pan heaters do work and can be left on all winter if installed properly. Other options are dipstick heaters that you just slip into the dipstick tube at night. the inline type that installs in the lower radiator hose and works by convection (install it in the engine compartment not up by the radiator). Or a combination of the above. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||12-27-1999||02:00 PM||||My uncle (who used to be a farmer) would put hay bales around his tractor, then fill a coffee can with a roll of kerosene soaked toilet paper and set it on fire under the oil pan.  It worked well until he set his tractor on fire, too.<P>  ****DO NOT TRY THIS ON A FIERO!!!!!****<P> This is just an observation.  The management is not responsible for any damage inflicted by stupid, idiotic morons who try this method on their cars.<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 12-27-1999).]||||reg||1
